  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The 60-day Islamabad MOU ceasefire expired today, August 17, but the two sides agreed an extension before the deadline — confirmed by Middle East Monitor (Aug 12), PBS, Anadolu Agency, and Yeni Safak. That agreement now frames the coming 31 days: the extension exists, but its durability is contested from within Iran before the ink is dry. Iran's Artesh spokesperson Brigadier General Mohammad Akraminia stated on August 2 — a quote amplified on state-affiliated platforms this week — that Tehran 'fully exploited' the MOU period to import new military equipment and repair damaged systems. No equivalent US or Iranian declaration of permanent diplomatic breakthrough accompanied the extension announcement. → supports <span class="cite">N5</span> without move</p>
      <p>Jared Kushner held a meeting with Hamas political leadership in Cairo on August 16, his first direct contact with the group, to advance Trump's 15-point Gaza road map. He is scheduled to meet Prime Minister Netanyahu in Tel Aviv today. Netanyahu's rejection of the road map last week — a rare public break with the Trump administration — drew condemnation from Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and other Gulf states, who said Israel 'now bears responsibility for obstructing peace.' Hamas has structural incentive to engage with the plan, at minimum conditionally, to deepen Netanyahu's isolation from Washington and Riyadh; a formal Hamas public response before EOD 2026-08-31 is the primary measurable on this track. → moved <span class="cite">N3</span></p>
      <p>The Strait of Hormuz remains near-closed. UKMTO put the week-of-August-17 transit rate at 17% of pre-conflict average. Bloomberg reported August 15 that Iran and Oman have 'homed in' on a formal transit framework, with Iranian co-management of inbound traffic and Omani oversight of outbound, but two blocking issues remain: Iran's demand for transit fees equivalent to approximately 5% of cargo value, which the US has formally rejected, and Iran's insistence that Washington end its naval blockade of Iranian ports as a precondition for any reopening. Trump complicated the diplomatic geometry on August 15 by stating he intends to declare Hormuz 'US territory' following Iran's defeat — a formulation that Iran's delegation in the Oman talks has characterized as a non-starter. → moved <span class="cite">N4</span>, <span class="cite">N5</span></p>
      <p>The IDF confirmed the killing of Hezbollah Radwan Force battalion commander Ali Samir Al-Haj Hassan in a targeted strike in southern Lebanon on August 15. Hezbollah confirmed the commander's death. Israeli military operations in Lebanon have continued throughout the MOU ceasefire period as a formally separate front; the US-Iran bilateral ceasefire framework has not been extended to cover Israeli-Lebanese operations, and neither side has cited the Lebanese strikes as a ceasefire violation in official communications. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Iran FM Abbas Araghchi stated on August 16 that Tehran 'has not decided whether to resume negotiations with the US,' days after a senior Iranian official told US News on August 12 that extension talks were not even occurring — directly contradicting the mediator-confirmed extension. The dual-track Iranian public posture — formal ceasefire extension through mediators, public non-commitment on talks through the FM — is consistent with a regime managing domestic hardliner pressure while preserving the diplomatic option. Whether Iran moves to a confirmed nuclear working-group session before EOD 2026-09-01 depends on whether the FM's public stance narrows to a named commitment or holds at deliberate ambiguity. → moved <span class="cite">N2</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:20%;">Escalation 20%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:65%;">Protracted 65%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:15%;">De-esc 15%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Ceasefire breaks (1 − N5 = 50%) and escalates to direct US-Iran strikes; the 'fully exploited the MOU' domestic framing and Trump's Hormuz sovereignty rhetoric provide the two most proximate triggers, weighted against the absence of carrier repositioning or NOTAM signals as of Day 171.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> The dominant path: ceasefire holds (N5 = 50%) but no nuclear working-group session emerges (1 − N2 = 70%), or ceasefire breaks but neither side escalates to declared military operations; Hormuz stays near-closed and diplomacy cycles through managed ambiguity.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Ceasefire holds (N5 = 50%) AND Iran publicly confirms a nuclear working-group session (N2 = 30%), creating an observable diplomatic breakthrough that anchors the extension beyond September 17; requires resolution of the Hormuz fee and blockade impasse as an enabling condition.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state broadcaster IRIB published the first video attributed to Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei since his installation in March 2026, an undated clip without identifiable location or date markers. The broadcast led with continuity messaging — characterizing the supreme leader as 'active and engaged' — without addressing his absence from public life now entering its sixth month. The regime's security apparatus appears to have established a pattern for the new supreme leader's communications: pre-recorded, released via state media, no live public appearance. The domestic audience receives this as normalized. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>On the ceasefire, state-affiliated channels Tasnim and Fars ran General Akraminia's 'fully exploited the MOU' statement multiple times through the August 10–16 period, each time without the diplomatic hedging typical of English-language official output. The frame offered to domestic audiences: Iran chose to fight the most powerful military alliance since World War II, accepted a tactical pause on its own terms, and used that pause to rebuild strength. Defeat is not part of the official vocabulary; the war is presented as a successful deterrence campaign that forced the United States into negotiations. → supports <span class="cite">N5</span> without move</p>
      <p>On the Hormuz track, IRNA and Tasnim consistently describe the Iran-Oman transit discussions as Iran exercising its lawful authority over the strait — with 'service charges' rather than 'tolls,' and 'traffic management' rather than 'access control.' Foreign Minister Araghchi's Aug 16 statement that Iran 'has not decided to resume negotiations' was carried without counter-framing domestically, with no counter-expectation set for resumed talks, as the regime's technocratic layer manages the actual diplomatic channel through back-channels. → supports <span class="cite">N2</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

